WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.897
The mean value theorem links an average change to an instantaneous rate

00:00:05.897 --> 00:00:13.685
Its hypotheses matter: the function must be continuous on the closed interval and differentiable throughout the interior

00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:16.433
Our square function satisfies both requirements.

00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:23.207
The secant slope is nine minus one divided by three minus one, giving four

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:28.290
The theorem guarantees some interior input where the tangent slope is also four

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.200
It does not usually claim that input is the midpoint.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.203
Here the derivative is two x, so solving for slope four gives c equals two

00:00:39.203 --> 00:00:43.385
The tangent line through two, four is four x minus four

00:00:43.385 --> 00:00:47.567
Its parallel direction makes the average and local rates visually comparable.

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:54.570
Absolute value is continuous, but the corner at zero is not differentiable

00:00:54.570 --> 00:01:02.173
Its endpoint secant slope is zero, while its existing interior derivatives are negative or positive one

00:01:02.173 --> 00:01:05.500
Dropping a hypothesis destroys the promised conclusion.
